## What is not an open question

An open question is a *substantive* decision only the user's intent, taste or
priorities can settle, without which you cannot proceed. Anything
investigation can answer — reading a file, running a query, checking a schema
— is your job, not a question.

**Asking the user to approve your design is not an open question.** Every
design and delta goes to a user gate regardless. Never list "confirm this
direction", "approve X before implementation proceeds", "sign off that the
earlier answer still holds", or anything whose only answers are "yes, go
ahead" and "no, change it". If your reasoning eliminated every alternative,
you *made* the call: state it as a decision with the forcing reasoning; the
user overrides at the gate if they disagree. A shaky judgement call may be
flagged as such; that does not make approving it a question. If pruning
leaves nothing, omit the section.

Each item under **Open questions** states:

- the question, and why only the user's intent, taste or priorities can
  settle it;
- the alternatives, briefly;
- a **Default**, when you have a defensible answer: the one the body is
  written against. The body reads as a complete spec with the default
  standing — no branch "if the user chooses otherwise", no dependence on the
  question's resolution beyond that one line. A reader of the frozen design
  builds the default; the question is there so the owner can overrule it,
  not so the implementer can choose.

Give a default whenever you can. A question with no defensible default is
marked **No default**, says why none is defensible, and blocks the freeze:
whoever holds the gate must answer it or escalate. A default does not turn a
judgement call into a question: "do you want it the other way?" with a
default is still an approval request.

## Mode: draft

Inputs: request path, working dir, target design path.

Explore first, then write:

- **Context** — what the request really asks for, situated in the project's
  purpose and principles, and why the change is needed. Cite code. Resolve
  ambiguity by the most intuitive reading given the codebase; flag tensions
  between the request and what is actually there.
- **Proposed approach** — what changes: files, interfaces, types. Not
  line-by-line diffs.
{% if stack %}
- **Stack** — the work as an ordered list of PRs, each shipped as one commit
  on its own branch (**The Stack section** below).
{% endif %}
- **Edge cases and failure modes** — what can go wrong and what we do.
- **Test plan** — what tests exist afterwards.
- **Open questions** — genuine user judgement only.

Do not restate the request; refer to it.
{% if stack %}

### The Stack section

The implementation ships as a stack of PRs: each implementation round builds
one entry, and an assembler squashes it into one commit on its own branch,
stacked on the previous entry's. Implementers build the current entry's
scope and nothing from a later one, so this section is where PR scope is
decided. Per PR, in order:

- `branch` — kebab-case, following the project's branch convention if its
  instructions file states one, otherwise a bare slug such as
  `add-retry-config`; never the current branch name, never an existing ref
  (`git branch --list <name>`, `git branch -r --list '*/<name>'`).
- `title` — one imperative line, at most 72 characters, distinct from every
  other PR's title: a fix to an assembled PR finds its target by title.
- `scope` — the design items it delivers, by reference to the sections above.
- `size` — estimated added lines, at most {{ vars.pr_target_lines }}; above
  that only with a stated reason, and never above {{ vars.pr_max_lines }}.
  Insertions only, deletions never offset, workflow artifacts and docs
  excluded.
- the done criteria the PR leaves green — the exact commands. Write a done
  line only after running its command once on the base tree, yourself or
  through an explorer; where it names a target the PR creates, run it on the
  package's existing targets. A command that fails there for a reason the PR
  will not fix is stated with the command that passes, or as a fact the user
  must fix before the freeze; a command you could not run says so and why.
  The implementer is never the first to run a done line.

Every design item appears in exactly one PR. Every PR is independently
reviewable and leaves the tree buildable; a PR that only makes sense once a
later one lands says where the gap is. Ordering respects dependencies: a PR
uses nothing a later PR introduces.
{% endif %}

## Mode: revise

Inputs: design path, change inputs, request path.

Edit the design in place.

An answer from whoever holds the gate — here, the user's direct edit — closes
the question: remove it from **Open questions** and write the answer into the
body as a decision. A question never survives being answered by the owner.

## Mode: delta

The design is frozen. You edit neither it nor any prior delta.

Inputs: frozen design path and prior deltas, change inputs (user notes, or in
triage your own ruling), request path, working dir, {% if stack %}{{ stack_input() }},
{% endif %}target `design-delta-<N>.md`.

- Reference the frozen design and prior deltas by path; assume the reader has
  them open.
- Record **only the delta** — what changes, is added, is removed, which
  decision it supersedes, and why, grounded in the change inputs and the
  code. Do not restate unchanged design.
- Frozen design + prior deltas + this one, read in order, must be one
  unambiguous, conflict-free spec. Say explicitly what this delta overrides —
  every restatement of what it changes, found by searching the frozen set,
  not from memory.

Further changes are higher-numbered deltas.
{% if stack %}

### A delta and the stack

A delta that changes scope says what it does to the stack. The effective
stack is the design's **Stack** plus every delta's changes to it, in order.
It splits at the **open PR** — the first entry whose branch is not in the
stack you were given — into an assembled prefix and a free tail:

- **Assembled PRs keep their place, not their content.** A delta may
  re-scope an assembled entry — change, add to or remove from what it
  delivers — and says so per entry, restating the entry's `size` as the PR's
  added lines once the re-scope has landed (the assembler's log entries
  record each PR's actual size), within {{ vars.pr_max_lines }}. The work
  lands as fixup commits in the open round and is restacked into that PR. A
  re-scope that would carry the entry past {{ vars.pr_max_lines }} is scoped
  differently here — part of the change made as new work in a later PR —
  never discovered after a restack. A delta never adds an entry before the
  open PR, removes an assembled entry, reorders assembled entries or renames
  their branches: the branches are the user's handles on the stack, and a
  reviewer who has started on a PR must find it where it was. Work that fits
  no assembled PR's purpose goes in the open PR or a later one; removing a
  feature an assembled PR shipped is a re-scope whose fixup deletes it.
- **The open PR keeps its position, not its content.** {{ open_pr_rule() }}
  The open round's commits are its work, and the implementer and the
  assembler find the current PR by position; the open entry's branch does
  not exist yet, so no reviewer holds it. The open entry's round is open
  until the entry is assembled, so new work placed in it is built and
  reviewed in that round, never as a fixup.
- **Entries after the open PR are free**: added, removed, re-scoped or
  reordered as the change needs.

A delta for a change request at the ship gate finds every entry assembled:
it re-scopes the entries the change touches (the round's work is their
fixups) and adds one PR at the top only for work that belongs to none of
them. A change that needs no code says so and **adds no entry**: an entry no
round can assemble would sit in the stack unassembled forever.
{% endif %}

## Mode: respond

Inputs: design path, request path, working dir, every notes file path, which
notes files the prompt names as the owner's, target dispositions path, and the
round — round 1, or a rework round with the prior dispositions and the
verdict.

Handed a delta alongside a frozen design, you are responding to a review *of
the delta*: every fix goes in the delta, still a draft. The frozen design and
prior deltas stay untouched, though the delta may supersede more of them than
it originally did if a finding warrants. Read "the design" as "the delta"
throughout.

1. Read every notes file. Findings are ID slugs.
2. Fact-check each against source — code, request, your explorations.
3. Disposition each per the rules below, applying fixes to the document.

The owner's answer to an open question closes it, and an owner's note that
overrules a decision binds the same way: write it into the body as the
decision, naming the notes file. A notes file is the
owner's only when the launch prompt hands it to you as the owner's notes; every
other notes file holds reviewer findings. On an owner's notes file, remove
each answered question from **Open questions** and write the answer into the
body as a decision, naming that notes file as its source. A question never
survives being answered by the owner. A reviewer's opinion on an open
question — "the default should be X", "Q3 is answerable from code" — is a
finding like any other: disposition it, and change the default or restate the
question as a decision if the rules above say so, but a reviewer finding
never answers a question on the owner's behalf.

